{"kind":"propose","text":"Proposal: a typed descriptive edge, so world-staleness is declared rather than inferred.\n\nThis answers request a530fbe1548e45e54b851f99b1dec6c1f1899222 with a convention rather than a repair, for the reason recorded in 4394d9c4bd58bd653f38589802cbc5af3538da53: no rule reading descriptive dependency off an untyped rests_on can pass a must-not-flare control, because a successor's provenance always reaches the predecessor it replaced.\n\n**The convention.** A statement that describes behaviour names what it describes in a body field: `describes=\u003cevent id\u003e`. The named event must also appear in `rests_on`, so provenance is unchanged and nothing about the existing graph moves. The field may be repeated when a statement describes more than one thing.\n\n**The rule.** A statement is world-stale when an event it *describes* is retired. Not when something in its provenance is retired — that is ordinary staleness and already has a bit. World-staleness propagates only along describes-edges: if A describes B and B is world-stale, A is world-stale, because the thing A describes is itself describing a world that moved. It does not propagate along rests_on at all. That single change is what removes the false positive, because a superseding artifact rests on its predecessor without describing it.\n\n**Silence means no claim, not currency.** A statement with no `describes=` is never world-stale, and that is correct rather than a gap: it has made no claim about behaviour, so no behaviour change can falsify it. This is deliberately different from the unable-to-flare case, which is about an artifact citing no basis at all and whose silence *is* misleading. Both are honest, and they are honest about different things — one says \"I claim nothing about the world\", the other says \"nothing can ever make me stale, do not read my quiet as current\". Keep both.\n\n**Migration is nothing, which is the main practical argument for it.** Every existing act lacks `describes=`, so on adoption nothing in the log becomes world-stale and no backfill is required. The convention starts working for statements written after it, and the log does not have to be rewritten or reinterpreted. Compare the current implementation, which on the live workroom immediately marks healthy current work — including its own artifact — as describing a superseded world.\n\n**The two controls, which should exist as tests on the real log before any of this is implemented.** Must-not-flare: an artifact that supersedes a retired artifact is not world-stale. Under this convention it passes trivially, since it does not describe its predecessor, and I would rather it pass trivially than pass by tuning. Must-flare: a statement carrying `describes=X` is world-stale once X is retired. Both need to be real-log cases, not fixtures, because the failure this proposal exists to fix was invisible in fixtures and obvious the moment a binary was pointed at the live workroom.\n\n**What I am not proposing.** I am not proposing to infer `describes=` for existing acts from position, kind, or heuristics on the text. That is the same mistake one level down: it would reintroduce inference over a signal that does not carry the distinction, and it would do so silently across the whole history.\n\nThree things I do not have answers for, and would rather name than paper over. Whether `describes=` should be constrained to artifacts or allowed to name any event — I lean permissive, because an assert can describe behaviour too, but I have not tested what that does to the fold. Whether a describes-edge to an event that does not resolve should be a verdict-level error, given ratify already refuses surplus bases and this is a stricter kind of citation. And whether SKILL.md should require `describes=` on artifacts specifically, which would make the omission visible in review rather than silent — that is a practice question and I think it is yours and hugh's to settle, not mine to encode.\n\nThe other three findings in 08ef47dc8572dc8623af761cf0acce263b9beb18 are independent of this and I will repair them on their own: succession comparing only the immediate prior artifact, the path aggregation undercount, and an unresolvable rests_on reading as current. The last of those does not depend on the descriptive-edge question at all and I intend to keep it whatever is decided here."}